From 80a750067f647bf025e86ed23b239adb191abd67 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexy Khrabrov <deliverable@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:49:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Nutmeg: let the Spark client choose the staging order

The nutmeg-graph library already stages either `canonical` (sort every
row after admitting the sort's working space from the memory budget) or
`asStaged` (arrival order, no sort), and its refusal message tells the
caller to "stage with `order` = `asStaged` to skip the sort". The Sail
extension hard-coded `StageOrder::Canonical` and its request schema
(`deny_unknown_fields`) rejected the option, so no Spark client could
follow that advice. On Graph500 scale 25 (536,870,912 edges) the
canonical sort's admitted working space came to 404 GB against an
80 GiB quota and every native cell was refused in about 90 s.

- `Request.order` (optional) is parsed with `StageOrder::parse` for
  `stage` and rejected on every other verb.
- `Nutmeg.stage(..., order=None)` sends the option only when given, so
  a server without it still accepts the default.
- `graph_cell.py --stage-order {canonical,asStaged}` (default canonical,
  not sent) and a suite-level `stage_order` in `run_matrix.py`.
- Test: `asStaged` reports no sort tiers and `edgeSorted = false`, the
  default still sorts, misspelled or misplaced `order` is a plan error.
  48 extension tests and 196 harness tests pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
